section.section_3_8
    h2 The Logic of War
    p
        |Elaborating the cultural worlds through which the young
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |passed makes possible a fuller understanding of the sources of the Soviet leader’s particular psychology, and the determinants of his personal and political trajectory. An understanding of the man comes from setting him in his time and place, even though some parts of his inner workings undoubtedly remain elusive, buried in regions to which historians are not admitted.
    p
        |Poverty was the condition of the world into which he came. The constant and demanding attention and fierce adulation focused on her son by
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Soso’s
        |&#32;
        |single present parent, his mother, fed his social resentment at his place in the scheme of things. Aware of being marginalized and at the same time talented,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Soso
        |&#32;
        |found that hard work and discipline moved him to the front of the class. His inheritance from both parents was ambition.
    p
        |The usual biographical narrative, with its organic continuity between boy, young adult, and mature man, is challenged by the reconstruction of the available fragments of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |&#32;
        |life. Those fragments suggest a much more disjointed evolution, in the course of which
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Soso/Koba/Stalin
        |&#32;
        |shed one identity and took on another. The obedient child and priest-in-training became a rebel; the Georgian patriot and romantic poet became an assimilated Russian — though only in part, and primarily in his public posture. The militant
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Blsh") Bolshevik
        |&#32;
        |at times took on the coloring of a moderate.
    p
        |As he moved physically and psychologically away from
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plGe") Georgia
        |,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |left behind a culture in which one’s sense of personhood derived from family, friends, and nation, and entered a world in which one defined one’s own nature in line with a particular understanding of historical direction and the unforgiving imperatives of politics. By becoming a rebel against the existing order, revolutionaries like
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |declared
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="CldW") war
        |&#32;
        |on constituted authorities.
    p
        |For Marxists in the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plRu") Russian Empire
        |, politics were less about compromise and persuasion, and more about the violent, unforgiving confrontations to be found on the battlefield. A logic of war prevailed, one that required (sometimes with regret) the use of violence.
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |responded both to inner needs and external possibilities and challenges, and was deeply changed by the experiences of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="OrCs") seminary
        |, underground, prison,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") revolution
        |,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="RuCW") civil war
        |, and political power. The figure that emerged on the world stage was simultaneously a product of the successive cultures through which he passed, and an actor making choices and defining himself in unprecedented and unpredictable historical circumstances.
    p
        |Delving deep into
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plGe") Georgia
        |&#32;
        |and its culture, I attempted carefully to construct a mosaic of diverse pieces of information — from the variety of archival documents, official and unofficial memoirs, even photographs — until a legible portrait of young
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |emerged. My aim was to evoke from that mosaic a credible and convincing interpretation of the evolution of the boy,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Soso Jughashvili
        |, through the young
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Koba
        |, to the mature
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |— an elucidation of his personal, psychological, and political formation that could shed light on his motivations and choices.
    p
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Jughashvili
        |&#32;
        |remains an elusive character, even to the most diligent scholar, but his early life provides important clues to the mysteries of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |and, hopefully, offers some understanding of why a
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") revolution
        |&#32;
        |committed to human emancipation ended up in dictatorship and terror.